{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-evidence` skill MUST be the home of the authoring workflow, fixing validation findings, outcomes and FIXME judgments, citation and site reviews, disagreeing assessments, and the commit message for a change to agreed requirement text.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only clause is that the `habeas-evidence` skill \"MUST be the home of\" seven topics. That clause says where documentation content lives. It prescribes no library, algorithm, signature, type or delegation. The roots are Markdown skill files, so there is no code structure to map. An alternative that left every topic stated in full in this skill but laid out the sections differently would still satisfy the clause, so section layout is discretionary.\n\nI checked that the supplied roots hold each topic:\n- Authoring workflow: `assets/skills/habeas-evidence/SKILL.md#section[Habeas%20evidence]/section[Workflow]`. Its steps cover `habeas author --all` and committing `evidence/` records.\n- Fixing validation findings: the `Fixing findings` section of `SKILL.md`. `references/authoring-failures.md` covers authoring failures, including configuration, credentials, throttling and denied reads.\n- Outcomes and FIXME judgments: the `Judgments` section of `SKILL.md` and `references/fixme.md#section[FIXME%20judgments]`.\n- Citation and site reviews: `references/reviews.md`, with its `Citation reviews` and `Site reviews` sections.\n- Disagreeing assessments: the `Disagreeing assessments` section of `SKILL.md`.\n- Commit message for a change to agreed requirement text: the `Rules` section of `SKILL.md`. A search excerpt shows it requires the reason and rationale in the commit message, described relative to the prior commit.\n\nThe other skills only point to this skill. `habeas-requirements` step 5 points to `habeas-evidence` \"Rules\" for the commit message. `habeas-refactor` points to the evidence skill and its \"Disagreeing assessments\". This does not duplicate a topic. Whether any topic is also stated in full in a second skill belongs to the parent requirement `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, not to this clause.\n\nMost of the text under these nodes came back elided, so I could not read it in full. My conclusions rest on the section structure and on the search excerpts for the Workflow, Rules, Disagreeing assessments and reviews sections.\n\nI found no structural obligation needing implementation evidence, and the roots hold the required topics.\n\nbehavioral: The subject requires the `habeas-evidence` skill to be the home of six topics. Under the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, each topic must be stated in full in exactly one skill. The topics are the authoring workflow, fixing validation findings, outcomes and FIXME judgments, citation and site reviews, disagreeing assessments, and the commit message.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_evidence_skill_is_home_of_authoring_findings_and_assessment_outcomes]` maps to these obligations as follows:\n- **Authoring workflow:** the `## Workflow` section must contain `habeas author --all`.\n- **Fixing findings:** `## Fixing findings` must contain the `| Finding | Action |` table and the `### Missing test and example references` heading.\n- **Disagreeing assessments:** `## Disagreeing assessments` must contain `The goal is`.\n- **Commit message:** `## Rules` must contain `in the message of the commit that makes it`.\n- **FIXME judgments:** `references/fixme.md` must contain `# FIXME judgments`.\n- **Citation and site reviews:** `references/reviews.md` must contain both `## Citation reviews` and `## Site reviews`.\n- **Outcomes:** `assert_home` checks the `habeas-evidence` markers from `home_markers`. These include `A judgment is one of three outcomes`, the FIXME judgment, citation review, site review, disagreeing assessment and commit message markers, and the `habeas author --all` and `## Fixing findings` markers.\n- **Exactly one home:** `assert_home` also checks that none of the other four skills in `SKILLS` contains any of those markers, using `bundled_skill`, which covers each skill's `SKILL.md` and reference files.\n\nThe assertions are real: `assert_text` panics when a phrase is missing, and `section` bounds each check to its heading. A skill that moved or dropped one of these topics, or restated it elsewhere, would fail the test. The marker phrases only approximate \"stated in full\", but that is as precise as a textual check of prose can be. I found no unmarked relevant test and no unacknowledged gap.",
